Denote by $h$ the height trapezoid $ABYX$, and $10-h$ in trapezoid $XYCD$.  Trapezoid $ABYX$ has bases of $12$ and $6$, and trapezoid $XYCD$ has bases of $12$ and $10$.  Therefore, the area of $ABYX$ is \[\frac{h~h(10+12)}{2}=\frac{h~11h}{2}\] and the area of $XYCD$ is \[\frac{(10-h)~h(10+12)}{2}=\frac{(10-h)~11(10-h)}{2}.\] By the Steiner-Lehmus theorem (https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Steiner-Lehmus_theorem), we can find the height $h$ by applying the Pythagorean theorem to the cool right triangle below.


[asy]
pair A, B, C, D, O, P, X, Y;
A= (6, 8);
B=(18, 8);
C=(24, 0);
D=(0,0);
O=(12,0);
P=(12,8);
X=(A+D)/2;
Y=(B+C)/2;
draw(X--Y, dashed);
draw(A--B--C--D--A);
draw(A--O--B);
draw(O--P, dashed);
label("A", A, NW);
label("B", B, NE);
label("C", C, SE);
label("D", D, SW);
label("O", O, S);
label("P", P, N);
label("X", X, NW);
label("Y", Y, NE);
draw((6,0)--(6,8), red);
draw((18,0)--(18,8), red);
label("h", (6,4), E);
label("12", (A+D)/2, NW);
label("12", (B+C)/2, NE);
draw((18,0)--(24,0)--(24,8)--(18,8)--cycle, blue);
label("6", (A+D)/2, NW);
label("6", (B+C)/2, NE);
draw((6,0)--(6,8), red);
label("8", (A+(A+D)/2)/2, NW);
label("4", (A+D)/2, NW);
label("8", (B+(B+C)/2)/2, NE);
label("4", (B+C)/2, NE);
draw(P--X--A--B--O--P, blue);
draw(P--X, dashed+red);
draw(X--Y, dashed+red);
draw(Y--C--O--Y, dashed+blue);
draw(A--X--O, dashed+red);
draw(P--X--P, dashed+red);
draw(O--Y--C, dashed+blue);
draw(Y--O--P, dashed+red);
draw(O---Y, dashed+red+blue);
label("12", (A+D)/2, NW+red);
label("12", (B+C)/2, NE+red);
label("10", (O+P)/2, W);
[/asy]Since $XO=PY=6$, let $PX=h_1$ and $OY=h_2$, and we have $h=h_1+h_2$.  Applying the Pythagorean theorem to the right triangle above gives \[h_1~h_2=h^2-6^2,\] while applying the Pythagorean theorem to triangle $XOP$ gives \[h_1~h_2=h^2-4^2,\] and applying the Pythagorean theorem to triangle $PYO$ gives \[h_1~h_2=h^2-8^2.\] Subtracting each of these equations from the original equation gives \[h_1~h_2=\frac{h^2-6^2}{2}=\frac{h^2-4^2}{3}=\frac{h^2-8^2}{5},\] which simplifies to $h=\boxed{6\cdot\sqrt{\frac{15}{2}}}$.

The area of $ABYX$ is then \[\frac{5(\sqrt{15})}{2}~\sqrt{\frac{15}{2}}=\frac{75}{\sqrt{2}},\] and the area of $XYCD$ is \[\frac{(10-\sqrt{15})~(\sqrt{15})}{2}

